"Who are the sandwich caregivers in Canada? (https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/pub/11-627-m/11-627-m2024003-eng.htm)\nciteturn2427view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/pub/11-627-m/11-627-m2024003-eng.htm\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 175\nL0:   * 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1:   * 【1†Skip to \"About this site\"】\nL2: \nL3: ## Language selection\nL4: \nL5:   * 【2†Français】\nL6: \nL7: 【3†/ Gouvernement du Canada†www.canada.ca】\nL8: \nL9: ## Search and menus\nL10: \nL11:   * 【4†Search and menus】\nL12: \nL13: ## Search\nL14: \nL15: Search Statistics Canada [Input] [Input: Search Statistics Canada]\nL16: \nL17: Search\nL18: \nL19: # Who are the sandwich caregivers in Canada?\nL20: \nL21: 【5†Image: Warning】 【6†View the most recent version】.\nL22: ## Archived Content\nL23: \nL24: Information identified as archived is provided for reference, research or recordkeeping purposes. It is not subject to the Government of Canada Web Standards and has not been altered or updated since it was archived. Please \"【7†contact us†www.statcan.gc.ca】\" to request a format other than those available.\nL25: ## Archived\nL26: \nL27: 【8†This page has been archived on the Web.】\nL28: \nL29: Release date: April 2, 2024\nL30: \nL31: 【9†More information】 【10†PDF version】\nL32: \nL33: 【11†Image: Infographic: Who are the sandwich caregivers in Canada?】 Description: Who are the sandwich caregivers in Canada?\nL34: \nL35: In 2022, 1.8 million (13%) of the 13.4 million Canadians aged 15 or older who provided unpaid care in the previous 12 months were sandwich caregivers.^{【12†Note 1】}\nL36: \nL37: ﻿\nL38: Table 1\nL39: Table summary\nL40: This table displays the results of Table 1 percent (appearing as column headers).\nL41:  | percent\nL42: Care for children and care-dependent adults/youth over 15^{【13†Note 2】}  | 13%\nL43: Care for children  | 52%\nL44: Care for care-dependent adults/youth  | 35%\nL45: \nL46: The family relationships between sandwich caregivers and care receivers are varied.\nL47: \nL48: ﻿\nL49: Table 2\nL50: Table summary\nL51: This table displays the results of Table 2 percent (appearing as column headers).\nL52:  | percent\nL53: Care for parents and children  | 34%\nL54: Care for parents and grandchildren  | 15%\nL55: Care for a spouse/partner and children  | 8%\nL56: Care for a spouse/partner and grandchildren  | 7%\nL57: \nL58: Sandwich caregivers were more likely to be of prime working age: 60% were 25 to 54 years of age.\nL59: \nL60: ﻿\nL61: Table 3\nL62: Table summary\nL63: This table displays the results of Table 3 percent (appearing as column headers).\nL64:  | percent\nL65: 15 to 24 years  | 7%\nL66: 25 to 34 years  | 11%\nL67: 35 to 44 years  | 29%\nL68: 45 to 54 years  | 20%\nL69: 55 to 64 years  | 18%\nL70: 65 to 74 years  | 13%\nL71: 75 years and older  | 2%\nL72: \nL73: Women were more likely than men to be sandwich caregivers.\nL74: \nL75: ﻿\nL76: \nL77: Table 4\nL78: Table summary\nL79: This table displays the results of Table 4 percent (appearing as column headers).\nL80:  | percent\nL81: Women  | 62%\nL82: Men  | 38%\nL83: Among sandwich caregivers, women were more likely than men to report negative impacts of caregiving on their…\nL84: \nL85: ﻿\nL86: \nL87: Table 5\nL88: Table summary\nL89: This table displays the results of Table 5 Men and Women (appearing as column headers).\nL90:  | Men  | Women\nL91: Health and well-being  | 75%  | 93%\nL92: Finances  | 28%  | 41%\nL93: Family relationships  | 38%  | 50%\nL94: \nL95: Source: Statistics Canada, Canadian Social Survey (Wave 6) – Well-being and Caregiving, 2022, and Canada’s Care Economy: A Conceptual Framework, November 2023.\nL96: ## Note\nL97: \nL98: Footnote 1.\nL99: \nL100: \nL101: In this infographic, “sandwich caregivers” are people who indicated in the Canadian Social Survey (Wave 6) – Well-being and Caregiving that they provided unpaid care to people under 15 years of age and people 15 years and older who had a long-term condition, disability or problem related to aging in the previous 12 months.\nL102: \nL103: 【14†Return to note 1 referrer】\nL104: \nL105: Footnote 2.\nL106: \nL107: \nL108: Children are those under the age of 15, while adults and youth are 15 years and over.\nL109: 【15†Return to note 2 referrer】\nL110: ## More information\nL111: \nL112: ### Note of appreciation\nL113: \nL114: Canada owes the success of its statistical system to a long-standing partnership between Statistics Canada, the citizens of Canada, its businesses, governments and other institutions. Accurate and timely statistical information could not be produced without their continued co-operation and goodwill.\nL115: ### Standards of service to the public\nL116: \nL117: Statistics Canada is committed to serving its clients in a prompt, reliable and courteous manner. To this end, the Agency has developed 【16†standards of service†www.statcan.gc.ca】 which its employees observe in serving its clients.\nL118: ### Copyright\nL119: \nL120: Published by authority of the Minister responsible for Statistics Canada.\nL121: \nL122: © His Majesty the King in Right of Canada, as represented by the Minister of Industry, 2024\nL123: \nL124: Use of this publication is governed by the 【17†Statistics Canada Open Licence Agreement†www.statcan.gc.ca】.\nL125: \nL126: Catalogue no. (number) 11-627-M\nL127: \nL128: Frequency: Occasional\nL129: \nL130: Ottawa\nL131: \nL132: Date modified:\nL133:     2024-04-02\nL134: \nL135: ## About this site\nL136: ### Statistics Canada\nL137: \nL138:   * 【18†Contact StatCan†www.statcan.gc.ca】\nL139:   * 【19†Trust Centre†www.statcan.gc.ca】\nL140: \nL141: ### Government of Canada\nL142: \nL143:   * 【20†All contacts†www.canada.ca】\nL144:   * 【21†Departments and agencies†www.canada.ca】\nL145:   * 【22†About government†www.canada.ca】\nL146: #### Themes and topics\nL147:   * 【23†Jobs†www.canada.ca】\nL148:   * 【24†Immigration and citizenship†www.canada.